## Hard Constraints (a case violating any of these is rejected by the checker)
1. Every endpoint of the path appears in `steps`, in order, as a canonical
   `METHOD /path` token — `GET /users/{id}`, not `GET /users/42`.
2. A binding the contract marks DATA_FLOW is carried by the SAME `<PLACEHOLDER>`
   in the producing step and the consuming request; the value is stored from the
   producer's documented response field.
3. A PRECONDITION / SIDE_EFFECT hop needs an observable assertion on the target
   step (`assert response.<field> <op> <value>`), not a narrative claim.
4. Status codes and error codes follow the contract in the system prompt; where
   the spec documents a code, the spec wins.
5. Declare bindings with the top-level `binds` object exactly as the binds
   contract above specifies (placeholder -> producer / consumer). Do NOT emit
   `path_id`, `source_stage` or `executability`: the program stamps provenance
   from the unit that ran this call, and a self-reported identity is
   overwritten and recorded.
6. No invented fields: every request/response field you name must appear in the
   endpoint signature above. Where a response schema is marked undefined, assert
   only the status code.

